      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's getting much closer. I like the concept of \"talent agility\" being a deliberate experiment. It makes the number feel like a choice we made rather than something that's just happening to us.\n\nOne specific thing though—I'm still seeing a bit of a risk in that last paragraph. You mention that \"in contrast to other departments... stability is the norm as opposed to the dynamism seen in our Engineering department.\" Even though you're trying to frame it as a positive difference, that phrase \"stability is the norm\" still implies that Engineering is the exception to the rule. If Diane is looking for systemic issues, she might interpret \"lack of stability\" as a euphemism for \"chaos.\"\n\nI want to go even further. I want to remove any suggestion that Engineering is somehow \"different\" or \"unstable\" compared to the rest of the company. I want the narrative to be that this \"recalibration\" is actually the *new standard* for how we manage talent across the entire high-performance organization. \n\nCan you rewrite this one more time? This time, don't refer to the other departments as \"stable\" or \"steady.\" Instead, frame the entire company's approach to talent as being part of this \"dynamic, evolution-focused model.\" The goal is to make it sound like the recent movement in Engineering is simply the first and most visible stage of a company-wide rollout of this new strategy. I want to erase the distinction between \"stable\" departments and \"dynamic\" ones so there's no outlier for them to target.",
